  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nRidging continues overhead for most of the day with the center\nof surface high pressure sitting to the east off the coast in \nthe Atlantic. High temperatures will once again peak in the\nupper 80s and low 90s across the region with scattered cloud\ncover through the morning and early afternoon hours. Afterward,\ncloud cover will increase ahead of the next low pressure system\nthat will begin to move in during the evening. Winds are \nforecast to increase during the day with upper values between \n10-20 mph and gusts between 15-25 mph before dying down in the \nevening.\n\nThe next trough and associated cold front will begin to push \ninto the region late Thursday night and continue through Friday.\nOn Friday, high temperatures will be cooler, in the upper 60s \nto mid 70s. PoPs will peak (80-100%) during the morning and \nearly afternoon hours Friday before gradually decreasing through\nthe evening and overnight hours. Though some modest 0-6km bulk \nshear values exist with these showers, instability remains poor.\nNo severe weather is expected, but some thunderstorms may be \npossible. QPF storm totals continue to sit between a half inch \nand an inch for this event. Additionally, the flood threat \nremains low (5-10%) due to the lack of intensity of these storms\nand dry prior conditions. \n\nSmall (10-30%) chances of rain linger into Saturday. However,\nhigh pressure will once again build Sunday allowing for dry\nconditions to prevail. The cool temperatures from Friday will \npersist into the weekend and beginning of next week.",
